"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[69483],{469483:e=>{e.exports="# ETL & Data Movement\n\nRedshift ETL patterns (`COPY FROM s3://...`, `UNLOAD`, scheduled workloads, materialized views) and transactional data movement (`BEGIN`/`COMMIT`, `LOCK TABLE`) → DBSQL equivalents.\n\n## Output format guidance\n\nChoose the output format based on the source artifact and the user's target:\n\n| Source                                                                   | Recommended output                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Redshift SQL statements / stored procedures                              | DBSQL code blocks (the normal migration output)                                          |\n| Redshift `COPY` / `UNLOAD` commands                                      | DBSQL `COPY INTO` / `CREATE TABLE AS SELECT` with a UC external location                 |\n| Scheduled Redshift script (e.g. via EventBridge, Airflow, Data Pipeline) | Prose mapping of pipeline steps → Databricks Workflow tasks, plus converted SQL per step |\n\nIf the user has not specified their target (Workflows vs DLT), ask before producing output — the two paths produce very different artifacts.\n\n## Redshift `COPY FROM 's3://...'` → DBSQL `COPY INTO`\n\nRedshift `COPY` is a bulk-load command from S3 (or DynamoDB, EMR, SSH). DBSQL's direct equivalent is `COPY INTO`, which loads from a Unity Catalog external location or volume.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift: CSV load\nCOPY sales FROM 's3://bucket/sales/2024/'\nCREDENTIALS 'aws_iam_role=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/RedshiftLoad'\nCSV IGNOREHEADER 1\nDELIMITER ','\nDATEFORMAT 'YYYY-MM-DD';\n\n-- DBSQL\nCOPY INTO my_catalog.my_schema.sales\nFROM 's3://bucket/sales/2024/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nFORMAT_OPTIONS (\n    'header' = 'true',\n    'delimiter' = ',',\n    'dateFormat' = 'yyyy-MM-dd'\n);\n```\n\n**Key translations:**\n\n| Redshift `COPY` clause                       | DBSQL `COPY INTO` equivalent                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CREDENTIALS 'aws_iam_role=...'`             | `-- TODO: manual review required — configure a Unity Catalog storage credential + external location for this IAM role (admin operation); then reference it via \\`COPY INTO ... WITH (CREDENTIAL credential_name)\\``                                                                                             |\n| `CSV` / `FORMAT CSV` / `FORMAT AS CSV`       | `FILEFORMAT = CSV`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `FORMAT AS PARQUET`                          | `FILEFORMAT = PARQUET`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `FORMAT AS JSON 'auto'`                      | `FILEFORMAT = JSON`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `FORMAT AS AVRO`                             | `FILEFORMAT = AVRO`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `FORMAT AS ORC`                              | `FILEFORMAT = ORC`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| `DELIMITER ','` / `DELIMITER AS ','`         | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('delimiter' = ',')`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `IGNOREHEADER 1`                             | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true')`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `DATEFORMAT 'YYYY-MM-DD'` / `TIMEFORMAT ...` | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('dateFormat' = 'yyyy-MM-dd', 'timestampFormat' = '...')` (Java patterns)                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `GZIP` / `BZIP2` / `LZOP`                    | Auto-detected from file extension; no explicit option needed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `COMPUPDATE OFF` / `STATUPDATE OFF`          | Drop — Delta updates statistics automatically                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `MAXERROR n`                                 | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mode' = 'PERMISSIVE', 'columnNameOfCorruptRecord' = '_corrupt')` — corrupt rows land in the `_corrupt` column; filter or monitor. No threshold that \"fails after N\" — use FAILFAST mode to fail on first bad record, PERMISSIVE to accept all.                                                |\n| `IGNOREBLANKLINES`                           | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('ignoreLeadingWhiteSpace' = 'true', 'ignoreTrailingWhiteSpace' = 'true')` for whitespace-only lines; blank rows are skipped by CSV parser default in most formats.                                                                                                                             |\n| `TRUNCATECOLUMNS`                            | No load-time truncation option. Wrap COPY INTO with a `SELECT` that applies `LEFT(col, N)` to each length-constrained column, or pre-define the target column as `STRING` (unconstrained) and truncate in a follow-up transform.                                                                                |\n| `MANIFEST`                                   | Use `PATTERN` (glob) in `COPY INTO FROM 's3://bucket/path/' PATTERN = '*.parquet'`. For a true manifest file (explicit list), load the manifest in a PySpark cell (`spark.read.json('s3://.../manifest.json')`), collect file URIs, then issue `COPY INTO ... FROM 's3://...' FILES = ('file1', 'file2', ...)`. |\n| `REGION 'us-west-2'`                         | Drop — bucket region is inferred from the external location                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n**Date/time pattern drift**: Redshift uses PostgreSQL patterns (`YYYY-MM-DD`, `HH24:MI:SS`). DBSQL uses Java `SimpleDateFormat` (`yyyy-MM-dd`, `HH:mm:ss`). Watch the `MM` vs `mm` trap for minutes — see `07-functions.md`.\n\n**Secondary COPY options** — less-common Redshift options and their DBSQL handling:\n\n| Redshift `COPY` option    | DBSQL handling                                                                             |\n| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `ACCEPTANYDATE`           | Pre-clean dates in a staging step; DBSQL has no \"accept-any-format\" date parser            |\n| `ACCEPTINVCHARS [AS '?']` | Set `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mode' = 'PERMISSIVE')` and inspect `_corrupt`; or sanitize upstream  |\n| `BLANKSASNULL`            | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('nullValue' = '')`                                                        |\n| `EMPTYASNULL`             | Same as `BLANKSASNULL` — `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('nullValue' = '')`                               |\n| `ENCODING 'UTF-8'`        | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('encoding' = 'UTF-8')` (default; specify only if non-UTF-8 source)        |\n| `ESCAPE`                  | CSV defaults handle `\\\\` as escape; override with `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('escape' = '...')`      |\n| `EXPLICIT_IDS`            | Drop — DBSQL identity column accepts explicit values when the column is in the column list |\n| `FILLRECORD`              | No DBSQL equivalent — pre-pad missing fields in a staging job                              |\n| `REMOVEQUOTES`            | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('quote' = '...', 'escape' = '...')` for CSV; default behavior otherwise   |\n| `ROUNDEC`                 | DBSQL truncates decimals on overflow; round explicitly in a follow-up `UPDATE` if needed   |\n| `TRIMBLANKS`              | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('ignoreLeadingWhiteSpace' = 'true', 'ignoreTrailingWhiteSpace' = 'true')` |\n| `NOLOAD`                  | Drop — validation-only; replicate by `COPY INTO` against a temp table                      |\n\n## Redshift `UNLOAD` → DBSQL export\n\nRedshift `UNLOAD` writes query results to S3. DBSQL has no one-liner `UNLOAD`; the equivalent is either a `CREATE TABLE AS` against an external location or a DataFrame write.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nUNLOAD ('SELECT * FROM sales WHERE year = 2024')\nTO 's3://bucket/sales-export/'\nCREDENTIALS 'aws_iam_role=...'\nFORMAT PARQUET\nPARTITION BY (region)\nALLOWOVERWRITE;\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: CTAS into a UC external table that lives at the target path\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.exports.sales_2024\nUSING PARQUET\nLOCATION 's3://bucket/sales-export/'\nPARTITIONED BY (region)\nAS SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.sales WHERE year = 2024;\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: write via PySpark in a notebook cell (when UNLOAD is inside a script)\n-- df = spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.sales WHERE year = 2024\")\n-- df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").partitionBy(\"region\").parquet(\"s3://bucket/sales-export/\")\n```\n\nThe Redshift inline ARN string `CREDENTIALS 'aws_iam_role=...'` has no direct DDL equivalent — configure the storage credential and external location in Unity Catalog separately. Once configured, reference via `WITH (CREDENTIAL credential_name)` if the output path requires explicit authorization.\n\n`ALLOWOVERWRITE` → `mode(\"overwrite\")` in the DataFrame form, or drop + recreate in SQL.\n\n**Secondary UNLOAD options:**\n\n| Redshift `UNLOAD` option     | DBSQL handling                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HEADER`                     | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true')` on CSV; Parquet ignores                        |\n| `NULL AS '...'`              | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('nullValue' = '...')`                                              |\n| `ESCAPE`                     | CSV default; override with `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('escape' = '...')`                      |\n| `ADDQUOTES`                  | `FORMAT_OPTIONS ('quoteAll' = 'true')` for CSV                                      |\n| `BZIP2` / `GZIP` / `ZSTD`    | Drop — DBSQL infers from file extension on write                                    |\n| `ENCRYPTED [AUTO]`           | Drop — UC external locations apply SSE-S3 (or KMS) per the configured credential    |\n| `KMS_KEY_ID '...'`           | Configure the KMS key on the UC storage credential, not inline                      |\n| `MAXFILESIZE [MB\\|GB]`       | Use `df.coalesce(n)` or `df.repartition(n)` in PySpark to control output file count |\n| `ROWGROUPSIZE [MB\\|GB]`      | Set `parquet.block.size` via session config or DataFrame write options              |\n| `MANIFEST [VERBOSE]`         | Use `_committed_*` Delta files or write a separate manifest in PySpark              |\n| `PARTITION BY (col) INCLUDE` | `PARTITIONED BY (col)` on CTAS; the partition column stays in output files          |\n| `REGION 'us-west-2'`         | Drop — bucket region is inferred from the external location                         |\n\n## Batch ETL pattern\n\n**Watermark table** — tracks the last-processed timestamp so each run only processes new rows. Create once before the first run:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS my_catalog.staging.watermark (\n    table_name STRING,\n    last_updated_at TIMESTAMP\n);\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.staging.watermark VALUES ('orders', TIMESTAMP '1970-01-01 00:00:00');\n```\n\n**Incremental load + MERGE** — the standard pattern per pipeline run:\n\n```sql\nTRUNCATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.orders;\n\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.staging.orders\nSELECT * FROM source_catalog.source_schema.orders\nWHERE updated_at > (SELECT last_updated_at FROM my_catalog.staging.watermark WHERE table_name = 'orders');\n\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.target_schema.orders t\nUSING my_catalog.staging.orders s ON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\n\n-- COALESCE guards against empty batches: if no new rows matched, MAX is NULL\n-- and a naive assignment would set the watermark to NULL, breaking every\n-- subsequent run because `updated_at > NULL` is always false.\nUPDATE my_catalog.staging.watermark\nSET last_updated_at = COALESCE(\n    (SELECT MAX(updated_at) FROM my_catalog.staging.orders),\n    last_updated_at\n  )\nWHERE table_name = 'orders';\n```\n\n## Scheduled jobs → Databricks Workflows\n\nRedshift workloads are scheduled via AWS EventBridge, Data Pipeline, Airflow, or external schedulers calling `RSQL` / `psql` scripts. The migration target is a **Databricks Workflow** where each step becomes a task:\n\n```\nExternal scheduler + RSQL script    →  Databricks Workflow\n├── Step 1: COPY staging_orders      →  SQL task: staging load\n├── Step 2: MERGE into target        →  SQL task: merge\n└── Step 3: UNLOAD audit             →  SQL task: CTAS to external location\n```\n\nSchedule → Workflow trigger (cron). Credential management → Unity Catalog storage credentials rather than inline `CREDENTIALS` clauses.\n\n## Materialized views with AUTO REFRESH\n\nDBSQL has a **native equivalent**: `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW ... SCHEDULE` on a Unity Catalog Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse. Translate directly:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW mv_daily_sales\nAUTO REFRESH YES\nAS\nSELECT sale_date, SUM(amount) AS total FROM sales GROUP BY sale_date;\n\n-- DBSQL (periodic schedule)\nCREATE OR REPLACE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.mv_daily_sales\nSCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOUR\nAS\nSELECT sale_date, SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.sales\nGROUP BY sale_date;\n\n-- DBSQL (cron — e.g. daily at midnight UTC)\nCREATE OR REPLACE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.mv_daily_sales\nSCHEDULE CRON '0 0 0 * * ?' AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'\nAS\nSELECT sale_date, SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM my_catalog.my_schema.sales\nGROUP BY sale_date;\n```\n\n`SCHEDULE EVERY N {HOUR|HOURS|DAY|DAYS|WEEK|WEEKS}` matches the cadence of Redshift's auto-refresh. Refreshes are incremental where the query shape supports it. For on-demand / manual refresh only, omit the `SCHEDULE` clause and call `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.mv_daily_sales` from a Workflow task.\n\n**Constraint**: materialized views require a UC-enabled Pro or Serverless SQL warehouse. If the target runtime is Classic or non-UC, fall back to DLT or scheduled `CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT` in a Workflow.\n\n## Transactions\n\n**Critical limitations:**\n\n1. **No nested transactions** — Redshift flattens nested `BEGIN` into a single transaction (with a warning); DBSQL does not support nesting at all. Flatten into a single block.\n2. **Limited DDL inside transactions** — DBSQL does not allow `CREATE`, `ALTER`, or `DROP` inside `BEGIN TRANSACTION ... COMMIT`. Move DDL outside the transaction.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nBEGIN;\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL (interactive)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL (recommended — auto-rollback on error)\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\nRedshift `BEGIN` (with or without `TRANSACTION` / `WORK`) → DBSQL `BEGIN TRANSACTION`. `END` as a transaction terminator (Postgres-style) → `COMMIT`.\n\n**Runtime note**: `BEGIN ATOMIC` requires DBSQL Serverless or DBR 18.0+. On earlier runtimes, use `BEGIN TRANSACTION ... COMMIT`.\n\n### Nested transactions (unsupported)\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift (flattened with a warning — inner BEGIN has no effect)\nBEGIN;\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    BEGIN;  -- warning: there is already a transaction in progress\n        INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\n    COMMIT;  -- does not commit yet\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL: flatten into a single ATOMIC block\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\n### LOCK TABLE\n\nRedshift `LOCK TABLE t` (acquires `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock for the current transaction) has no DBSQL equivalent. DBSQL MVCC is always non-blocking for readers; writers serialize automatically at commit time.\n\nRemove `LOCK TABLE` statements. If the source relied on the lock to serialize a read-then-write pattern, rewrite using:\n\n- **`MERGE`** — atomic read + write in one statement.\n- **Optimistic concurrency** — read, compute, write; on conflict retry. Delta will fail the commit with a `ConcurrentModificationException`-equivalent if another writer has modified the same rows.\n\n### Isolation levels\n\nBoth dialects default to **snapshot isolation** (MVCC) — Redshift switched its default to SNAPSHOT for provisioned clusters and serverless workgroups; DBSQL has always used snapshot semantics. Behavior matches for most read/write patterns. If the source explicitly used `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE` to opt into stricter guarantees, flag it: DBSQL has no runtime override for isolation level, and serializable-specific anomalies (write-skew) may appear differently.\n\nDrop `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...` — DBSQL does not support runtime isolation-level changes.\n\n### DML return values\n\n| Redshift                                     | DBSQL                                               | Notes                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `pg_last_copy_count()` / `pg_last_copy_id()` | No direct equivalent                                | Inside SQL scripting: `GET DIAGNOSTICS v = ROW_COUNT` after the `COPY INTO` |\n| `GET DIAGNOSTICS var = ROW_COUNT` (PL/pgSQL) | `GET DIAGNOSTICS var = ROW_COUNT` (DBSQL scripting) | Identical                                                                   |\n"}}]);
